<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fukushima Masanori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1561">1561</a>–<a href="/years/1624">1624</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fukushima Masanori was a Japanese warlord who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> and fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> in 1600. Ieyasu rewarded him with Aki and Bingo provinces, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>. In 1619 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> stripped him of the domain for repairing Hiroshima Castle without permission, and the Asano family replaced him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori.jpg" alt="Fukushima Masanori" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masanori_Fukushima.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fukushima Masanori, first called Ichimatsu, was born in 1561 at Futatsudera in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, the eldest son of the barrel merchant Fukushima Masanobu, though some sources suggest his real father was Hoshino Narimasa, a cooper from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiyosu">Kiyosu</a>. His mother was the younger sister of the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, who was thus his first cousin, and as a young man he served Hideyoshi as a page. He first saw battle in the assault on Miki Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harima-province">Harima Province</a> from 1578 to 1580.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shizugatake">Battle of Shizugatake</a> in 1583 Masanori took the first head, that of the enemy general Ogasato Ieyoshi, and became known as one of the Seven Spears of Shizugatake, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-kiyomasa">Katō Kiyomasa</a>. After the Kyūshū Expedition of 1587 he was made a daimyō with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of Imabari in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyo-province">Iyo Province</a>, rated at 110,000 koku. In the Korean campaign he captured Ch'ongju in 1592, and in 1595 he led 10,000 men to surround Seiganji Temple on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-koya">Mount Kōya</a> until Toyotomi Hidetsugu had killed himself, after which he received Hidetsugu's former fief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiyosu">Kiyosu</a>.</p><p>After Hideyoshi's death in 1598 Masanori was one of seven generals who moved against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishida-mitsunari">Ishida Mitsunari</a>, who had underreported their achievements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. Popular accounts call it a plot to kill him, while the historian Watanabe Daimon sees a legal dispute that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> mediated. In the campaign of 1600 Masanori helped take Takegahana Castle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gifu-castle">Gifu Castle</a>, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a> he led the Eastern Army's advance guard, opening the battle with a charge against Ukita Hideie's army in one of its bloodiest clashes, which turned his way when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a> changed sides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The charge against him was that, by repairing part of Hiroshima Castle damaged by a typhoon flood, he had broken the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buke-shohatto">Buke Shohatto</a>. He had applied for permission two months earlier without receiving it. The matter was first settled on condition that he apologise and remove the repairs, but the bakufu then held the removal insufficient and confiscated his lands in Aki and Bingo, worth 500,000 koku. He was given instead the Takaino Domain, worth 45,000 koku, in Shinano and Echigo. He died on 26 August 1624. His descendants became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatamoto">hatamoto</a> in the service of the Tokugawa shōgun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fukushima Masanori” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Mori were reduced to Choshu after Sekigahara, Tokugawa Ieyasu gave Aki and Bingo to <b>Fukushima Masanori</b> in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/state/hiroshima-domain">Hiroshima Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Futatsudera. Died in Takayama, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.68,138.36&amp;year=1601" aria-label="Takayama on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8025 841 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Fukushima Masanori"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8475 1111h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8475 1111h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=36.68,138.36&amp;year=1601">What was here in 1601? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
